menu "PandaStatusOS"

config PS_LED_COUNT
    int "LED count on the bar (CONFIRMED)"
    default 25
    range 1 255
    help
        Still not counted. It was 8, and on the hardware 8 lit about a quarter of the bar,
        so the bar holds roughly 32. Sending data for more LEDs than exist is harmless, the
        surplus runs off the end of the strip; sending for fewer leaves the rest dark, which
        is what 8 did. Effects are drawn across this many, so if the true count differs a
        progress bar fills to slightly the wrong end. Set it to the counted number when
        someone counts.

config PS_LED_GPIO
    int "LED data GPIO (CONFIRMED)"
    default 5
    help
        Unknown until the board is traced. Set from the hardware note, not from a guess.

config PS_WS_MAX_CLIENTS
    int "Concurrent WebSocket clients"
    default 4

endmenu

config PS_LED_PIN_WALK
    bool "Walk the candidate LED GPIOs at boot instead of rendering (diagnostic)"
    default n
    help
      The bar's data pin is PROVISIONAL, and a wrong pin looks exactly like a dead strip.
      With this on, the firmware does nothing but drive each candidate pin in turn, blinking
      the strip a number of times equal to that pin's position in the list. Whoever is
      watching counts the blinks and the count names the pin. Never ship this on.

config PS_LED_FANOUT
    bool "Fan the LED signal out to every safe pin (OBSOLETE)"
    default n
    help
      The bar's data pin has never been established. A wrong pin looks exactly like a dead
      strip, so this routes the RMT output to every pin the chip can drive without disturbing
      the flash, the USB or the console, and the bar lights wherever it really is. Turn this
      off and set PS_LED_GPIO once the pin is known: one pin driven beats eleven.
